Acknowledgements

This project's design borrows openly, and says so in the files where it does:

  * The AI-control pattern the trusted monitor implements - allow, defer to a
    trusted model, escalate to a human - follows Greenblatt et al.,
    "AI Control: Improving Safety Despite Intentional Subversion"
    (arXiv:2312.06942).

  * The control/data separation between the shopper and the payer is the
    property CaMeL enforces with a custom interpreter; here it falls out of the
    token chain instead.

  * The graph layout, the node-per-module structure and the structured-chain
    idiom follow the RabbitHole project's courtroom, which several modules cite
    directly.

  * Cumulative spend enforcement exists because the Agent Interaction Protocol
    specifies that it is the runtime's responsibility and not the token's.
